    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 21st looking to capitalize on favorable platoon matchups, with **Kazuma Okamoto** emerging as our most reliable play. Sporting a 65.31 efficiency score, **Kazuma Okamoto** is positioned in the cleanup spot against the lefty Rod\u00f3n; his plate discipline against southpaws, combined with the favorable park factor, makes him the cornerstone of today's lineup. Similarly, **Dillon Dingler** (60.31) provides high-floor value from the three-hole. While his base projection is modest, his dynamic platoon advantage against the lefty Cantillo gives him a significant boost, making him our strongest point-per-dollar catcher today. Rounding out the active trio is **Carson Benge** (57.23), whose role as the leadoff hitter against the mid-tier arm Cavalli offers high run-scoring potential, even if he remains just shy of the \"Strong\" start threshold.\n\nOur bench strategy remains hampered by scheduling, as impact bats like **Yordan Alvarez**, **Junior Caminero**, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, and **Trea Turner** are unavailable due to team off-days. With limited roster depth active today, we are effectively playing a short bench; consequently, we are proceeding without **Kyle Schwarber**, **Christian Yelich**, **Caleb Durbin**, **Hyeseong Kim**, **Colt Keith**, **Zac Veen**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Luke Raley**, **Josh Smith**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena**. No difficult bench decisions were required today, as the lack of games for our core secondary hitters mandated a defensive posture to preserve our weekly game caps. The primary risk remains the potential for lineup volatility; since **Dillon Dingler**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, and **Carson Benge** are all subject to shifting order assignments, we must monitor official lineups to ensure they maintain their projected spots, as any drop in the batting order could immediately degrade their efficiency scores.",
